  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO\nCOUNTIES...\n\n\n...UPDATED AVIATION DISCUSSION...\n\nThe showers ended last evening and skies have cleared out quite\ncleanly early this morning. With clear skies in the valleys\nallowing for maximum radiational cooling, there should be some\npatches of ground fog early this morning to go with the chilly\ntemps, dipping into the 40s for many valleys. Skies today will be\nquite sunny, but with increasing high clouds late today and\ntonight. A trough of low pressure will move west to east to the\nnorth of SoCal Tuesday into Wednesday. This will enhance onshore\nbreezes, especially for the mountains, deserts, and coastal waters\nduring this time, but only to around 25-30 mph. It will also \nregenerate some marine layer low clouds for the coast and valleys.\nLater Wednesday into Friday, high pressure aloft amplifies more \nmeaningfully, producing a noticeable warming trend for Thursday \ninto the weekend. With colder air occupying the interior West at\nthe same time, some offshore flow will develop for SoCal, but at \nthis time offshore winds do not appear strong or otherwise \nproblematic, especially through Friday. However, right now it \nlooks like the weekend could have stronger offshore winds, but \nfor now that is a low confidence idea. High temperatures through \nWednesday will continue several degrees below average for this \ntime of year, mostly in the 70s at lower elevations, low 80s for \nthe low deserts. Lows will be mild, actually a few degrees above \nseasonal averages in the mid 40s and 50s. Highs and lows will \nincrease by the weekend, and the offshore flow will change the \ndistribution of the highest temperatures. That means lots of 80s \nwest of the mountains, even close to the coast. Some upper 80s\neven look probable for inland coastal areas and western valleys, \nif all goes to plan.",
